Right now the update checker relies on the hash of the current `.jar` file being used. This means that any file not uploaded to Modrinth (in my case, dev builds), will not trigger the update checker. I had originally assumed that Mod Menu used the version field in `fabric.mod.json`, but it does not.

Therefore, I suggest adding a new metadata entry:
```json
{
  "custom": {
    "modmenu": {
      "modrinth_id": "<PROJECT ID HERE>",
      "update_strategy": "semver" // If you think this behavior should have to be explicitly enabled
    }
  }
}
```

In my case, the project id would be either `"nbt-editor"` or `"5Osk0m1G"`. Mod Menu could then check for updates by comparing the standard version field from `fabric.mod.json` to the latest `version_number` from Modrinth with semver, which would be more robust. Note that the current mod version may not be able to be mapped onto a Modrinth release (in my case, the version would be `<next version>-dev`, and there is no release with that version number), so comparing release dates may not be possible (which is why I suggest relying on semver).

While I realize Mod Menu already supplies an API for providing a custom update checker, I figure that this is likely a common enough problem that it would be helpful if Mod Menu supported this directly, since it already does something very similar.

While not necessary, note that the Modrinth project id could be scraped from the homepage link (`"homepage": "https://modrinth.com/mod/nbt-editor"`) or mod id (`"id": "nbteditor"`) (though in my case the mod id is missing the dash, so this one wouldn't work). This would allow this system to work on mods that haven't specifically gone ahead and setup the Mod Menu stuff.

When a config contains a `List<T>` of some nested config class `T`, it does not appear in the mod menu. For example:

```
@Modmenu(modId = MOD_ID)
@Config(name = "modconfig", wrapperName = "ModConfig")
public class ModConfigModel {
    public static class NestedConfig {
        public NestedConfig (String name, boolean enabled) {
            this.name = name;
            this.enabled = enabled;
        }
        public String name;
        public boolean enabled;
    }

    public String testString = "testString";

    @Expanded
    public List<NestedConfig > list = List.of();

    @Nest
    public NestedConfig  nested = new NestedConfig ("conf", false);

    public List<String> testStrings = List.of("test");
}
```

`testString`, `nested`, and `testStrings` all appear in the mod menu, but `list` does not.

